From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] theme: white ink and a stroke for frost/glass windows, and keep it when tiled

Two owner reports, one root cause each, in the same file.

Window headers never read Luma's appearance mode. --headerbar-fg-color and
--headerbar-bg-color are native GTK custom properties (_colors.scss, :root),
and nothing in this series ever varied them by luma-treatment. Settings'
surface-treatment panel writes G_DESKTOP_COLOR_SCHEME_DEFAULT for light,
frost and glass alike -- only its own dark toggle writes prefer-dark -- so
frost and glass windows loaded the *light* native GTK theme's headerbar
colours: dark ink on a near-white bar. Exactly the class of bug the Shell's
own menus had (gnome-shell patch 0152, same root cause, same session), and
exactly the direction the owner asked reversed: "On LIGHT, a dark font is
fine. On FROST and ESPECIALLY glass I think it needs to be white with drop
shadow." The title label, the app-menu identity button, and the header-bar
icons all inherit color from the one ancestor rule (headerbar { color:
var(--headerbar-fg-color); }), so one override at window.csd.luma-treatment-*
reaches all three.

Frost and glass now define --headerbar-bg-color from the same smoked veil the
window frame itself uses (config/shared/design-tokens.json,
surface_treatments.frost/glass.frame) and --headerbar-fg-color: #ffffff, with
one text-shadow and -gtk-icon-shadow -- the same shadow token the Shell ledger
uses (js/ui/lumaSurfaceMaterials.js) -- so a window reads as the same material
as the shelf above it. window.csd.luma-treatment-* is two classes on the
window, which outranks :root's specificity regardless of source order. Light
is untouched: the native values already read correctly there, and this file's
own light gradient still applies.

Tiled windows lost their stroke along with their outer shadow. 0032 bundles
the per-treatment inset stroke and the three outer-elevation shadow layers
into one box-shadow list, and zeroes the whole property for .maximized,
.fullscreen and every .tiled* class together. 0036's own commit message says
tiled windows "still draw no outer shadow (0032)" -- true for maximized and
fullscreen, which meet the screen edge and want none, but for tiled it also
means no stroke at all. On glass, that stroke is what separates the window
from the wallpaper and from the tile beside it, so its absence is visible the
moment a window is snapped into a tile and gone the moment it's pulled back
out.

Maximized and fullscreen are unchanged. Tiled windows get their own
per-treatment rule restoring only the inset stroke -- no outer shadow --
using the exact alphas the luma-no-frame-shadow pattern already established
for this purpose (0.82/0.045/0.62/0.48 light/dark/frost/glass), so a tiled
window now reads exactly like a floating one with its drop shadow turned off.
It is inset, so it does not grow the window's footprint or encroach on the
gap between two adjacent tiles.

Both changes verified by compiling gtk.scss with sassc against a from-scratch
reconstruction (pristine libadwaita-1.9.3 plus this series' own patches
0001-0037 in order) and confirming the new rules survive compilation with the
expected specificity ordering; no other rule in the 6900-line output changed.

Generated against the app-menu-highlight tree (0037 applied) for release .47.
---
diff --git a/src/stylesheet/widgets/_window.scss b/src/stylesheet/widgets/_window.scss
--- a/src/stylesheet/widgets/_window.scss
+++ b/src/stylesheet/widgets/_window.scss
@@ -118,6 +118,63 @@
     outline: none;
   }
 
+  // A tiled window still meets a gap, not the display edge, so it keeps the
+  // one-treatment inset stroke that separates it from the wallpaper and from
+  // its neighbour tile -- only the outer elevation shadow drops, the same way
+  // it already does for luma-no-frame-shadow. These are the same per-treatment
+  // alphas that pattern already uses (0.82/0.045/0.62/0.48), so a tiled window
+  // reads exactly like a floating one with its drop shadow turned off.
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-top:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-left:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-right:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-bottom:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-top:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-left:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-right:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-light.tiled-bottom:backdrop:not(.fullscreen) {
+    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
+  }
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-top:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-left:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-right:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-bottom:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-top:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-left:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-right:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-dark.tiled-bottom:backdrop:not(.fullscreen) {
+    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
+  }
+  // Frost and glass need this stroke the most: it is what separates a smoked,
+  // wallpaper-tinted surface from the wallpaper and from the tile beside it.
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-top:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-left:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-right:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-bottom:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-top:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-left:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-right:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-frost.tiled-bottom:backdrop:not(.fullscreen) {
+    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.62);
+  }
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-top:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-left:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-right:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-bottom:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-top:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-left:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-right:backdrop:not(.fullscreen),
+  &.csd.luma-treatment-glass.tiled-bottom:backdrop:not(.fullscreen) {
+    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.48);
+  }
+
   &.solid-csd {
     margin: 0;
     padding: 5px;
diff --git a/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss b/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss
--- a/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss
+++ b/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss
@@ -94,8 +94,64 @@
   min-height: 24px;
   padding: 0;
   border-radius: 10px;
+}
+
+// The native --headerbar-bg-color/--headerbar-fg-color custom properties
+// (_colors.scss, :root) never varied by Luma's own appearance mode -- only by
+// GTK's native color-scheme, which Settings' surface-treatment panel writes
+// as `default` for light, frost AND glass alike (only its own dark toggle
+// writes prefer-dark). So frost and glass windows loaded the *light* native
+// theme's headerbar colours: dark ink on a near-white bar, the same class of
+// bug the shell's own menus had (see gnome-shell patch 0152) and exactly the
+// direction the owner asked to be reversed. `window.csd.luma-treatment-*` is
+// two classes on the window, so it outranks :root's single-pseudo-class
+// specificity and wins regardless of source order.
+//
+// Light keeps the native values (dark ink already reads correctly there, and
+// this file's own light gradient above already tints the bar). Frost and
+// glass become the same smoked veil the window frame itself uses
+// (config/shared/design-tokens.json, surface_treatments.frost/glass.frame),
+// with white ink and the one shadow every smoked Luma surface uses
+// (gnome-shell's ledger, js/ui/lumaSurfaceMaterials.js) -- so a window reads
+// as the same material as the shelf above it.
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost {
+  --headerbar-bg-color: rgba(24, 29, 36, 0.84);
+  --headerbar-fg-color: #ffffff;
+}
+
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass {
+  --headerbar-bg-color: rgba(12, 17, 24, 0.76);
+  --headerbar-fg-color: #ffffff;
+}
+
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost headerbar,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass headerbar {
+  // The light gradient above is scoped to prefers-color-scheme: light, so it
+  // never reaches frost/glass; this only has to stop the contrast-mode
+  // hairline from reading as a light-surface edge on a dark one.
+  @media (prefers-contrast: more) {
+    box-shadow: inset 0 -1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.24);
+  }
 }
 
+// White ink on a smoked, wallpaper-tinted veil needs the shadow to hold over
+// a busy wallpaper -- the same reasoning and the same token as the shelf.
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost windowtitle .title,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass windowtitle .title,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost headerbar .luma-identity-button > button,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass headerbar .luma-identity-button > button {
+  text-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(6, 9, 13, 0.80);
+}
+
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost headerbar windowcontrols button > image,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass headerbar windowcontrols button > image,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost headerbar .luma-identity-icon,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass headerbar .luma-identity-icon,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-frost headerbar .luma-identity-chevron,
+window.csd.luma-treatment-glass headerbar .luma-identity-chevron {
+  -gtk-icon-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(6, 9, 13, 0.80);
+}
+
 headerbar .luma-identity-button > button {
   min-height: 24px;
   padding: 5px 8px 5px 4px;
